ion, turning left from south gives you east. So why doesn’t that work at the pole? Because at a normal location, south is a specific direction (toward the pole). At the pole itself, **every horizontal direction is south**. So “turn left from south” is ambiguous: which south? If you define south as “toward London,” turning left gives you a direction that is also south (toward Siberia), but that new direction is **also south** — it’s not perpendicular to the original direction in the horizontal plane. In fact, at the pole, all horizontal directions are **radially outward**. Turning left from one radial line gives you another radial line, not a line tangent to a circle. ### The critical geometric fact On a sphere, at any point **except the poles**, the set of directions can be divided into: - **Meridians** (north-south lines) - **Parallels** (east-west lines) These are perpendicular. At the poles, meridians all meet, and parallels shrink to a point. So **one of the two perpendicular sets disappears**. You can only move along meridians (south), not along parallels (east/west). **So turning left at the pole changes which meridian you’re on, not whether you’re moving south. That’s why it’s not east — east would require moving along a parallel, which doesn’t exist there.** ### A final test: Walk it If you turn left from facing London (south) and then take one step, where do you go? You go **south** along the Siberia meridian. You haven’t gone east at all. If you instead wanted to go east, you would need to step in a direction that is **perpendicular to all meridians** — which, at the pole, is impossible because the only horizontal directions *are* meridians. I hope this helps clarify why turning left doesn’t create east at the pole, even though it does everywhere else. The geometry of the sphere breaks down at that single point.
