e pole, it's a **mathematical convention** that assigns east to the direction of increasing longitude. If you try to walk that way, you will still move **south** (initially), not east. ### So, why does it *seem* like a compass should show east/west? Because at any other place, turning left or right from north gives you west/east. At the pole, there is no horizontal "north," so the rule breaks. A compass that *does* show east/west at the pole is using a fictional north (like grid north) to make the numbers work — but that doesn't reflect the physical reality of directions. **Bottom line:** - A real magnetic compass will **not** show east/west at the pole — it will spin erratically or point downward. - An electronic compass that *does* show east/west is using a convention that has no physical meaning for walking. You are still facing south, no matter what the display says.
